--------------------------------------------------
Conditions / Constraints
--------------------------------------------------

    - Maximum 64 instructions (imem has 64 slots)
    - Every program MUST end with fc000000 (HALT)
    - Supported instructions only:
        R-type : add, sub, and, or, slt
        I-type : addi, lw, sw, beq
        J-type : j
    - lw and sw use word-aligned addresses only (multiple of 4)
    - dmem has 64 word slots — addresses beyond 0xFF will wrap or fail
    - $zero (register 0) is hardwired to 0 — writes to it are ignored
    - No floating point, no multiply/divide, no syscall

--------------------------------------------------

The memfile (memfile.dat) contains the hex-encoded MIPS program
loaded into instruction memory at simulation start.

IMPORTANT: Every program must end with FC000000 (HALT instruction).
The testbench waits for HALT to reach Writeback before printing PMU results.

--------------------------------------------------
Current program: 10-instruction benchmark
--------------------------------------------------

Assembly:
    addi $s0, $zero, 5      # $s0 = 5
    addi $s1, $zero, 3      # $s1 = 3
    add  $s2, $s0, $s1      # $s2 = 8
    sub  $s3, $s0, $s1      # $s3 = 2
    sw   $s2, 0($zero)      # mem[0] = 8
    lw   $t0, 0($zero)      # $t0 = 8   <- load-use stall
    add  $t1, $t0, $s1      # $t1 = 11  <- stall here
    beq  $s3, $s1, +1       # NOT TAKEN (2 != 3)
    addi $t2, $zero, 7      # $t2 = 7
    addi $t3, $zero, 99     # $t3 = 99  
    FC000000                # HALT

Expected PMU output:
    Instructions Retired : 10
    CPU Cycles           : 15
    CPI                  : 1.50
    Memory Accesses      : 2
    Branch Count         : 1
    Mispredictions       : 0
    Load-Use Stalls      : 1
    Prediction Accuracy  : 100.0%
    Pipeline Efficiency  : 93.3%


To run a different program:
    Replace the hex values in memfile.dat.
    Always end with fc000000.
    Recompile and simulate
        vvp sim
